6176945 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176946 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176946 #edb/ref 1061 8778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176946 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eaf23fc9-d311-5210-b4e5-83bf29c1cd0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176946 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176947 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176947 #edb/ref 1061 8780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176947 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe14b5e5-3934-58ec-925b-8f9569cfa306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176947 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176966 #edb/ref 1064 "Two angles are **congruent** if their measures are equal.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nm\\angle X =50^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\ \\quad m\\angle Y=50^\\circ,\n$$\n\nthen $\\angle X$ and $\\angle Y$ are congruent. We can express this as \n\n$$\n\\angle X \\cong \\angle Y.\n$$\n\nHowever, if \n\n$$\nm\\angle X =50^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\ \\quad m\\angle Z = 110^\\circ,\n$$\n\nthen $\\angle X$ and $\\angle Z$ are *not* congruent, and we write \n\n$$\n\\angle X \\ncong \\angle Z.\n$$\n\nWe represent congruent angles by marking the arcs with the same symbol. For example, three pairs of congruent angles are shown below:\n\n![](images/99/997e53a399f06c3aad8391ace4172b7249890a31abeff4e72605bce607fd3bdb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176966 #edb/ref 1065 2184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "927a2746-94e0-5f78-985c-38429a67496b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176966 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179639 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180062 #e